<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tripitaka Koreana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1236">1236</a>–1251</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Palman Daejanggyeong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tripitaka Koreana is a complete Buddhist canon carved on 81,258 wooden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> blocks by the Goryeo court between 1236 and 1251, in the hope of divine protection against the Mongol invasions. It replaced an earlier set burned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1232. Kept at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haeinsa">Haeinsa</a> temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, it is the most complete canon in Chinese script and is listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tripitaka-koreana.jpg" alt="Tripitaka Koreana" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tripitaka_Koreana_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
